const intro={"n":24,"title":"Picardía Defends His Vote and Is Punished by the Police","nums":[925,927,929],"summary":"Picardía continues his account of the police officer who persecuted him. During a contested election the officer demands that he surrender his voting list and support the candidate backed by the committee. Picardía insists he will vote for whomever he chooses. Police strike him with sabers, and he is confined in a cepo. He concludes bitterly that justice follows the advantage of the cunning and powerful. These events are told by Picardía, whose earlier gambling dishonesty remains part of his characterization.","people":"Picardía as first-person narrator; the hostile police officer nicknamed ñato; police at the election; a judge and election committee mentioned in the account.","themes":"Electoral coercion; political freedom; authority and retaliation; the gap between justice and power; an ethically complicated narrator.","words":"eleciones (vernacular spelling of elecciones, elections); lista (voting list); candilato (candidato, candidate); Comiqué (Comité, political committee); cepo (confinement device); ñato (officer's nickname).","questions":["What does the officer demand of Picardía at the election?","How does Picardía answer when told whom to vote for?","What happens when the police intervene?","Why does the comparison between playing cards and voting matter?","What parts of the account require distinguishing Picardía's words from independent historical evidence?"],"analysis":"The scene sharply opposes a demand for controlled voting and Picardía's claimed right to choose his candidate. The narrator's prior deception at cards adds tension to his defense of fair dealing. The canto is literary testimony about coercion, not proof of every allegation against the judge.","related":"/en/students/vuelta-canto-23/","relatedTitle":"Picardía's earlier conflict with the officer"};
